<!-- Section 1: Introduction -->
<section id="arcsin-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Find an Angle Using Sine</h2>
  <p>Picture a wheelchair ramp: you know its sloping surface measures 9.5 metres and it climbs 0.75 metres from the ground. What is the ramp's incline angle? The right triangle angle from sine calculator answers exactly this kind of question. It takes the opposite side and the hypotenuse of a right triangle and returns the acute angle between them.</p>
  <p>These two measurements are sufficient because sine is defined as the ratio of the opposite side to the hypotenuse. Once that ratio is known, inverse sine converts it back into the angle. The adjacent side never enters the calculation, which makes this the right tool whenever the opposite side and hypotenuse are the quantities you actually measured.</p>
  <p>Throughout this guide, <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> is the acute reference angle being calculated, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the side opposite angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. The calculated angle is the tilt or slope where the hypotenuse meets the adjacent side, measured in degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Understanding the triangle -->
<section id="arcsin-anatomy" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding the Opposite Side and Hypotenuse</h2>
  <h3>What Is a Right Triangle?</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has one angle of exactly 90 degrees. The other two angles are acute, meaning each is strictly less than 90 degrees, and together they always add up to 90 degrees. This complement relationship is why finding one acute angle immediately gives you the other by subtraction.</p>
  <p>The right-angle condition matters because sine, cosine, and tangent are defined through the fixed side ratios of right triangles. Without the 90-degree corner, the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> has no fixed relationship to any angle, and inverse sine cannot recover one.</p>
  <h3>What Is the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>The opposite side is the side directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. It never touches the vertex of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. In height problems this side usually represents vertical rise: the height a ladder reaches up a wall, the rise of a ramp, or the vertical component of a crane boom.</p>
  <p>The label is relative. If you switch your reference angle from <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> to <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} />, the side that was opposite becomes adjacent and vice versa. Always confirm which angle you are solving for before labelling sides.</p>
  <h3>What Is the Hypotenuse?</h3>
  <p>The hypotenuse is the side opposite the right angle, represented by <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />. It is always the longest side of the triangle. It never touches the right-angle vertex. In practical settings the hypotenuse is often the sloping length itself: the ladder, the rafter, the ramp surface, or the straight-line sight distance.</p>
  <p>When a triangle is drawn rotated, identify the hypotenuse first by finding the side across from the square right-angle marker. Everything else follows from that identification.</p>
  <h3>What Is the Adjacent Side?</h3>
  <p>Side <span set:html={rich('[[b]]')} /> is the leg that touches angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> without being the hypotenuse. This calculator does not need it. If you later require the adjacent side, the Pythagorean theorem gives it as <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c^2 - a^2}]]')} /></p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('arcsin-anatomy.svg')} alt="Right triangle with opposite side a and hypotenuse c highlighted for arcsin" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Anatomy for arcsin: side a lies opposite angle A, and side c is the hypotenuse across from the right angle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Understanding sine -->
<section id="arcsin-what" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is Sine in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Sine measures what proportion of the hypotenuse is represented by the opposite side. The definition is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A)]]')} /> is the sine of angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} /> is the opposite side, and <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} /> is the hypotenuse. If the opposite side is 3 units and the hypotenuse is 5 units, then <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A) = 3/5 = 0.6]]')} />. The ratio is dimensionless: metres divided by metres cancel, so the same 0.6 results whether you measure in metres, feet, or centimetres.</p>
  <h3>What Is Inverse Sine?</h3>
  <p>Ordinary sine starts with an angle and produces a ratio. Inverse sine starts with a valid ratio and returns the corresponding angle. If <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A) = 0.6]]')} />, then <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(0.6) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Inverse sine reverses the sine relationship to recover the angle you started from.</p>
  <h3>Is Arcsine the Same as Inverse Sine?</h3>
  <p>Yes. The notations <span set:html={rich('[[\\arcsin(x)]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^{-1}(x)]]')} /> refer to the same function. The superscript <span set:html={rich('[[-1]]')} /> does not mean reciprocal. The reciprocal of sine is a different function entirely: cosecant, written <span set:html={rich('[[\\csc(x)]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Why Does the Formula Use the Opposite Side?</h3>
  <p>Sine is the ratio that pairs the opposite side with the hypotenuse, which is exactly the pair this calculator accepts. The memory aid SOH-CAH-TOA summarises the three pairings: SOH means Sine equals Opposite over Hypotenuse, CAH means Cosine equals Adjacent over Hypotenuse, and TOA means Tangent equals Opposite over Adjacent. When your known sides are opposite and hypotenuse, SOH applies, so arcsin is the correct inverse function.</p>

  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('arcsin-ratio.svg')} alt="Diagram showing divide a by c then apply arcsin to get the angle" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">How arcsin works: divide the opposite side by the hypotenuse, then apply inverse sine to recover the angle.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Deriving the formula -->
<section id="arcsin-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Inverse Sine Formula Works</h2>
  <p>Start with the sine definition:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\sin(A)=\\frac{a}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The known side lengths let you compute the ratio on the right. Then apply inverse sine to both sides to isolate the angle:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[A=\\arcsin\\left(\\frac{a}{c}\\right)]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Inverse sine is applied to the entire ratio because the ratio as a whole is the sine value. Work through a numerical example. Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} /> cm:</p>
  <p>Step 1: Divide the opposite side by the hypotenuse: <span set:html={rich('[[3/5 = 0.6]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Apply inverse sine: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(0.6)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Calculate the angle: <span set:html={rich('[[A \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>The result is an approximation rounded to two decimal places. In plain language: the angle whose sine is 0.6 measures about 36.87 degrees.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: How to use -->
<section id="arcsin-howto" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Angle From Sine Calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Identify the right angle.</strong> The calculator only works for right triangles, so locate the 90-degree corner first.</li>
    <li><strong>Locate the opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />.</strong> It lies directly across from angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /> and never touches its vertex.</li>
    <li><strong>Identify the hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c]]')} />.</strong> It sits opposite the 90-degree angle and is the longest side.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure or obtain both side lengths.</strong> Use a tape, a plan drawing, or given problem data.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the opposite-side length</strong> into the corresponding input field.</li>
    <li><strong>Enter the hypotenuse length</strong> into its input field.</li>
    <li><strong>Select compatible units</strong> with the unit selectors so both sides use the same unit.</li>
    <li><strong>Check that the hypotenuse is longer than the opposite side.</strong> If it is not, the sides are mislabelled.</li>
    <li><strong>Press Calculate Angle A.</strong> The tool divides, applies arcsin, and returns the angle.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the calculated angle in degrees</strong> and note whether it looks reasonable for the triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Verify the result</strong> with one of the checks in the verification section below.</li>
    <li><strong>Use Reset</strong> to clear the inputs and start another calculation.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Foundational examples -->
<section id="arcsin-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Examples of Finding an Angle Using Sine</h2>
  <h3>Example 1: A Basic 3-4-5 Right Triangle</h3>
  <p>Given opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> cm and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} /> cm, find angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 1: Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(a/c)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 2: Substitute the values: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(3/5)]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 3: Calculate the ratio: <span set:html={rich('[[3/5 = 0.6]]')} /></p>
  <p>Step 4: Apply inverse sine: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(0.6) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} /></p>
  <p>This angle is the tilt between the hypotenuse and the adjacent side, with the 3 cm side standing opposite it.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: When the Opposite Side Is Half the Hypotenuse</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> m: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(5/10) = \\arcsin(0.5) = 30^\\circ]]')} /> exactly. This is the familiar 30-60-90 special triangle, where the side opposite the 30-degree angle is half the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: A Triangle With an Opposite-to-Hypotenuse Ratio of 0.8</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 8]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> m: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(8/10) = \\arcsin(0.8) \\approx 53.13^\\circ]]')} />. The angle is larger than 30 degrees because the opposite side represents a larger proportion of the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: A Small Opposite Side</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[a = 2]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> ft: <span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(2/10) = \\arcsin(0.2) \\approx 11.54^\\circ]]')} />. A relatively short opposite side produces a small angle, as the triangle is long and shallow.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: Real-world applications -->
<section id="arcsin-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Real-Life Examples of Calculating Angles Using Sine</h2>
  <h3>Example 5: Finding a Roof's Incline Angle</h3>
  <p>A roof rafter is 6.5 m long and rises 4 m vertically above the level of its starting point. Find the angle between the rafter and the horizontal. The rafter and the vertical rise form a right triangle: the rise is opposite the incline angle, and the rafter is the hypotenuse. Inverse sine is appropriate because the known pair is opposite and hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(4/6.5) = \\arcsin(0.61538) \\approx 37.98^\\circ]]')} />. The roof climbs at roughly 38 degrees from horizontal. Note this geometric calculation is not a complete roof-design assessment; structural factors are separate.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Finding the Angle of a Ladder</h3>
  <p>A ladder is 5.5 m long and reaches 4.5 m up a wall. Calculate the angle between the ladder and level ground. The wall height is opposite the ground angle, and the ladder itself is the hypotenuse, so arcsin applies.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(4.5/5.5) \\approx 54.90^\\circ]]')} />. The ladder leans at about 54.9 degrees. This calculation alone does not establish whether the ladder is safely positioned.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Calculating a Ramp's Incline</h3>
  <p>A ramp has a sloping length of 9.5 m and a vertical rise of 0.75 m. Find its angle relative to the horizontal. The sloping surface is the hypotenuse and the vertical rise is opposite the incline angle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(0.75/9.5) \\approx 4.53^\\circ]]')} />. The ramp rises at a gentle 4.53 degrees. Do not claim that the calculated ramp meets accessibility or building-code requirements.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Finding an Angle in Surveying</h3>
  <p>A simplified surveying measurement records a vertical difference of 30 m and a straight-line sloping distance of 50 m. Calculate the angle between the sloping line and the horizontal. The vertical difference is opposite the angle; the sloping distance is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(30/50) = \\arcsin(0.6) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} />. Real surveying can require instrument-height corrections and terrain adjustments beyond this simplified model.</p>
  <h3>Example 9: Calculating a Guy Wire's Angle</h3>
  <p>A guy wire is 18 m long, with a vertical separation of 10 m between its endpoints. Find the angle between the wire and the horizontal. The vertical separation is the opposite side and the wire is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(10/18) \\approx 33.75^\\circ]]')} />. The result describes geometry only; it does not determine wire tension, anchor strength, or structural safety.</p>
  <h3>Example 10: Finding the Incline of a Staircase</h3>
  <p>A simplified staircase has a vertical rise of 2.8 m and a sloping stringer length of 5 m. Calculate the angle between the stringer and the horizontal. The rise and the stringer are the opposite side and hypotenuse respectively.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(2.8/5) = \\arcsin(0.56) \\approx 34.06^\\circ]]')} />. This calculation does not establish building-code compliance.</p>
  <h3>Example 11: Calculating an Aircraft's Climb Angle</h3>
  <p>For a simplified flight segment, an aircraft gains 1,500 m in altitude while travelling 5,000 m along a straight flight path. Estimate the climb angle relative to the horizontal. Altitude gain is opposite the climb angle; the flight path is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(1500/5000) = \\arcsin(0.3) \\approx 17.46^\\circ]]')} />. This is a straight-segment model, not a full analysis of a changing flight path.</p>
  <h3>Example 12: Finding the Angle of a Mechanical Brace</h3>
  <p>A mechanical brace has a length of 1,700 mm and a vertical rise of 800 mm. Calculate its angle relative to the horizontal. The formula works identically in millimetres: the rise is opposite, the brace length is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(800/1700) \\approx 28.07^\\circ]]')} />. The result describes geometry, not structural strength.</p>
  <h3>Example 13: Calculating a Crane Boom's Incline</h3>
  <p>A simplified crane boom has a length of 10 m and a vertical component of 8 m. Find the angle between the boom and the horizontal. The boom length is the hypotenuse and the vertical component is opposite the angle.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(8/10) = \\arcsin(0.8) \\approx 53.13^\\circ]]')} />. Do not treat the result as establishing crane capacity or operational safety.</p>
  <h3>Example 14: Estimating a Tree's Angle of Elevation</h3>
  <p>A person observes the top of a tree from a point where the vertical difference to the treetop is 12 m and the straight-line sight distance is 20 m. Find the angle of elevation. The vertical difference is opposite the angle; the line of sight is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(12/20) = \\arcsin(0.6) \\approx 36.87^\\circ]]')} />. This gives the angle from the observation level; it does not automatically provide the tree's total height above ground.</p>
  <h3>Example 15: Calculating a Hiking Route's Incline</h3>
  <p>A simplified straight hiking section has a sloping length of 100 m and a vertical rise of 80 m. Find the angle of incline. The rise and the sloping distance are the opposite side and hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(80/100) = \\arcsin(0.8) \\approx 53.13^\\circ]]')} />. This is a steep illustrative slope; actual hiking routes vary in gradient along their length.</p>
  <h3>Example 16: Finding an Awning Support's Angle</h3>
  <p>A diagonal awning support is 3.5 m long and rises 1.2 m vertically. Calculate the angle between the support and the horizontal. The vertical rise is opposite the angle and the support is the hypotenuse.</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[A = \\arcsin(1.2/3.5) \\approx 20.05^\\circ]]')} />. The result does not verify the support's structural suitability.</p>

