<!-- Section 8: Ratio affects angle -->
<section id="arcsin-ratio" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How the Opposite-to-Hypotenuse Ratio Changes the Angle</h2>
  <p>The ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> determines angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. A larger opposite side relative to the hypotenuse produces a larger acute angle, because more of the hypotenuse's length stands vertical. A small ratio produces a small angle: the triangle is long and shallow.</p>
  <p>When the opposite side is exactly half the hypotenuse, the angle is 30 degrees. Proportional triangles share angles regardless of size: scaling every side by the same factor leaves every ratio unchanged, so the angles stay fixed. Changing units from metres to centimetres changes nothing for the same reason.</p>
  <p>The ratio must stay between 0 and 1 for an ordinary right triangle. A ratio of 0 would mean no opposite side at all, and a ratio of 1 would mean the opposite side equals the hypotenuse; both are degenerate boundary cases, not real triangles with three positive sides.</p>
