<!-- Section 9: Units -->
<section id="arcsin-units" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Angle Units and Measurement Precision</h2>
  <p>This calculator returns angles in degrees, the standard unit for construction, surveying, and classroom geometry. A degree divides a full circle into 360 parts. Radians are the alternative: a full circle is <span set:html={rich('[[2\\pi]]')} /> radians. Inverse sine on a handheld calculator can return either, depending on its mode setting.</p>
  <p>Convert between them with: <span set:html={rich('[[\\text{Degrees} = \\text{Radians} \\times \\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}]]')} />. The input side ratio itself has no unit, and the two sides must use compatible units: metres with metres, or millimetres with millimetres.</p>
  <h3>Example: Does the Measurement Unit Change the Angle?</h3>
  <p>Take <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} /> m. The ratio is 0.6 and the angle is 36.87 degrees. In centimetres, <span set:html={rich('[[a = 300]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 500]]')} /> cm give the same ratio 0.6 and the same angle. Scaling both measurements by the same factor cancels out.</p>
  <p>Keep extra digits during intermediate steps and round only the final angle. The justified precision depends on the input measurements: rough field measurements support one decimal place, while precise workshop measurements may support two.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 10: Mistakes -->
<section id="arcsin-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating an Angle Using Sine</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Using the adjacent side instead of the opposite side.</strong> Check which vertex your angle sits at, then take the side across from it. If you used the touching leg, you computed arccos, not arcsin.</li>
    <li><strong>Using the hypotenuse as the numerator.</strong> The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} />, opposite over hypotenuse. Flipping it gives a ratio above 1 and a domain error.</li>
    <li><strong>Reversing the ratio.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\arcsin(c/a)]]')} /> is not the same angle. For 3-4-5, <span set:html={rich('[[\\arcsin(3/5) = 36.87^\\circ]]')} /> but <span set:html={rich('[[\\arcsin(5/3)]]')} /> is undefined.</li>
    <li><strong>Using ordinary sine instead of inverse sine.</strong> Sine needs an angle input. If you already know the sides and need the angle, you need the inverse function.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing inverse sine with reciprocal sine.</strong> <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin^{-1}(x)]]')} /> is arcsin; <span set:html={rich('[[1/\\sin(x)]]')} /> is cosecant. They are unrelated.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering an opposite side longer than the hypotenuse.</strong> Geometrically impossible in a right triangle. Relabel your sides: the longest measurement is the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li><strong>Entering zero or negative side lengths.</strong> Lengths must be positive. A zero opposite side describes a flat line, not a triangle.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixing measurement units.</strong> Entering the opposite side in metres and the hypotenuse in centimetres corrupts the ratio. Convert first.</li>
    <li><strong>Rounding the ratio too early.</strong> Rounding 0.61538 to 0.62 before applying arcsin shifts the roof example from 37.98 to 38.32 degrees. Keep full precision until the end.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporting radians when degrees are expected.</strong> An answer of 0.64 usually means the calculator was in radian mode; multiply by <span set:html={rich('[[180/\\pi]]')} /> to get 36.87 degrees.</li>
    <li><strong>Calculating angle B when the tool finds angle A.</strong> The calculator returns the angle opposite side <span set:html={rich('[[a]]')} />. The other acute angle is <span set:html={rich('[[90^\\circ - A]]')} />.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming the angle verifies structural safety.</strong> The angle is pure geometry. Ladder stability, roof strength, and ramp compliance need separate engineering checks.</li>
    <li><strong>Confusing vertical rise with sloping distance.</strong> In the ramp example, 9.5 m is the sloping surface (hypotenuse), not the horizontal run. Swapping them changes the triangle entirely.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating an impossible ratio as valid.</strong> If your computed <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> exceeds 1, stop: no right triangle has those sides. Find the labelling error instead of forcing a calculation.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: Validation -->
<section id="arcsin-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Check Whether Your Calculated Angle Is Valid</h2>
  <p>A valid, non-degenerate right triangle requires: the opposite side positive, the hypotenuse positive, the hypotenuse strictly longer than the opposite side, the ratio <span set:html={rich('[[a/c]]')} /> strictly between 0 and 1, and the resulting angle strictly between 0 and 90 degrees.</p>
  <p>If <span set:html={rich('[[a = c]]')} />, arcsin returns 90 degrees, a degenerate limiting case rather than a real triangle. If <span set:html={rich('[[a > c]]')} />, the triangle is geometrically impossible.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Check the sine ratio</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(A) \\approx a/c]]')} /> using the unrounded angle. For the 3-4-5 example, <span set:html={rich('[[\\sin(36.87^\\circ) \\approx 0.6 = 3/5]]')} /></p>
  <h3>Method 2: Check the complementary angle</h3>
  <p>Compute <span set:html={rich('[[B = 90^\\circ - A]]')} />. The two acute angles must sum to exactly 90 degrees. Then confirm the triangle makes sense with angle <span set:html={rich('[[B]]')} /> as reference.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Use the Pythagorean theorem</h3>
  <p>Compute the adjacent side <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c^2 - a^2}]]')} />, then verify <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 \\approx c^2]]')} />. For <span set:html={rich('[[a = 3]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 5]]')} />: <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{25 - 9} = 4]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[9 + 16 = 25]]')} />. Small discrepancies come from rounding the displayed angle, not from the calculator.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: Choosing -->
<section id="arcsin-choose" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Choosing the Right Right Triangle Calculator</h2>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Sine Calculator:</strong> use when you know the opposite side and the hypotenuse and need angle <span set:html={rich('[[A]]')} />. This is the page you are on.</p>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Cosine Calculator:</strong> use when you know the adjacent side and the hypotenuse. Same angle, different known pair.</p>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Angle From Tangent Calculator:</strong> use when you know both legs but not the hypotenuse. Common for rise-over-run field measurements.</p>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Angle Calculator:</strong> helps you pick the right method when you are unsure which sides you have.</p>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Side From Sine Calculator:</strong> reverses the direction: given an angle and the hypotenuse, it finds the opposite side.</p>
  <p><strong>Right Triangle Side Calculator:</strong> finds missing side lengths from known triangle measurements.</p>
  <p>Match the tool to your measurements. If the opposite side is unknown and hard to measure, do not compute it just to use arcsin; pick the inverse function that fits the sides you already have.</p>
</section>

